#ntdiff PRIVATE/NTOS/BOOT/LIB/SCSIDISK.C PRIVATE/NTOS/BOOT/LIB/I386/ARCEMUL.C PRIVATE/NTOS/FW/DUOBASE/MIPS/SCSIDISK.C
+/*++
=1 5 3
+    sdcard.c
=1 9 3
+    This module implements the hard disk boot driver for the Raspberry Pi
+    (the ARM BOOT\LIB\SCSIDISK.C ScsiDiskEntryTable) over its SD card, the
+    system disk. The card is disk 0 of SCSI adapter 0, the adapter the
+    configuration tree describes: scsi(0)disk(0)rdisk(0).
+
+    The BCM2836 and BCM2837 have two card controllers: the SD host controller
+    (SDHOST, 0x202000 into the peripherals), a small Broadcom core, and the
+    EMMC (0x300000), an Arasan SD host controller (SDHCI 3.0). The VideoCore
+    firmware boots from the card on the SDHOST, with the slot's GPIO 48-53 at
+    function ALT0, and connects the EMMC to the wireless chip of the Pi 3 and
+    Zero 2 W on GPIO 34-39 (ALT3). The card stays on the SDHOST here, so that
+    the EMMC is free for the wireless driver; the EMMC (GPIO 48-53 at ALT3) is
+    tried only when no card answers on the SDHOST. Every BCM2711 board (Pi 4)
+    wires its card to the EMMC2 (SDHCI 3.0, 0x340000), which has pins of its
+    own; QEMU's raspi4b attaches it to the EMMC instead, which is looked at
+    when the EMMC2 has no card.
+
+    Both controllers are driven by programmed I/O: each 512-byte block passes
+    through a data port. Card bring-up follows the SD physical layer
+    specification: CMD0, CMD8, ACMD41, CMD2, CMD3, CMD9 (capacity), CMD7,
+    ACMD6 (4-bit bus). A standard capacity card is byte addressed, a high
+    capacity card block addressed. The card code is shared; a controller is a
+    set of routines (SD_HOST): reset, clock, command, response, transfer and
+    bus width.
+
+    SDHOST: a command goes to SDCMD with the NEW flag, which clears when the
+    response is in SDRSP0-3 (a long response keeps its CRC byte, which the
+    SDHCI drops), and its FAIL flag and SDHSTS report errors. Data moves
+    through a 16-word FIFO whose level SDEDM shows. There is no automatic
+    CMD12: a multiple block transfer is ended with one. The SD clock is the
+    VideoCore core clock divided by SDCDIV + 2; as the core clock may change
+    with load, the divisor is taken from its maximum rate.
+
+    EMMC: the EMMC loses a register write that follows another within two SD
+    clocks (a clock domain crossing), so every register write but the data
+    port's is followed by a delay longer than two clocks at the 400 kHz
+    identification clock.
=1 23 3
